#: includes/Admin/Page.php:52
msgid "About all files"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:52
msgid "Every file found in wp-content/uploads, linked to a Media Library item where possible. Sizes are read from disk, so they include every generated thumbnail and any other file in the folder."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:53
msgid "About unused files"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:53
msgid "Not referenced anywhere in the database, in the active theme files or in text files inside uploads. This is checked again right before deleting. Links from plugin code, other sites, newsletters or emails cannot be detected. Revisions, trashed posts and the \"uploaded to\" parent do not count as use."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:54
msgid "About orphaned files"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:54
msgid "Files on disk that are not part of any Media Library item, such as leftover sizes of deleted items, zips, sql dumps, logs and plugin folders. They can still be in use, for example hard-coded in content."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:55
msgid "About orphaned and unused files"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:55
msgid "The safest group to clean up: not in the Media Library and not referenced anywhere. Plugin cache folders (for example elementor/css) are regenerated by their plugin when needed."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:56
msgid "About originals"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:56
msgid "WordPress keeps the full size upload next to the -scaled file it actually serves. An original only counts as used when something links to it directly. Deleting an unused original keeps the Media Library item working: regenerating thumbnails falls back to the -scaled file, the image editor already uses the -scaled file and the frontend never uses the original. You lose the full resolution file itself (for downloads or print) and sizes larger than the -scaled file (default 2560px on the longest side)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:57
msgid "About WebP copies"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:57
msgid "Copies made by optimizer plugins (SiteGround Optimizer, EWWW and others) as foo.jpg.webp or foo.webp. WordPress does not know about them, so they follow the usage of their source image. Deleting them while the optimizer is active means it serves the original instead or regenerates them."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:60
msgid "About main files"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:60
msgid "The main file is the one WordPress serves, often the -scaled version. Deleting a main file deletes the whole Media Library item, including all its sizes."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:61
msgid "About generated sizes"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:61
msgid "Generated sizes are the thumbnails WordPress creates for an item. They follow the usage of that item."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:64
msgid "About used files"
msgstr ""

#: includes/Admin/Page.php:64
msgid "The \"Where used\" column shows what references the file. Used files are locked and cannot be deleted unless \"Also delete files marked as used\" is checked."
msgstr ""

